WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.760
It’s easy to pick the best and worst lines in&nbsp;
Game of Thrones. The true challenge lies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:09.440
going through the landfill that is the last two&nbsp;
seasons of the show and trying to find the gems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:15.720
amidst the garbage. So I’m going to try my best&nbsp;
to pick the “greatest” lines in seasons 7 and 8.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:21.200
Number 20: “Respect is how the young keep us&nbsp;
at a distance, so we don't remind them of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:30.120
unpleasant truth. Nothing Lasts.” So initially&nbsp;
I thought this line was pretty deep and clever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:35.120
but then I realized that it doesn’t seem that&nbsp;
realistic. I don’t know anyone who necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.840
distances themselves from old people and this&nbsp;
line kind of shines a negative light on them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.840
Like sure, they can remind you of the unpleasant&nbsp;
truth of aging. But they also offer so much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.680
like wisdom that comes from that age. From a more&nbsp;
pessimistic perspective, this line rings true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.560
so I’ll give it that at least and it’s obviously&nbsp;
better than the absolute mediocrity of the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.760
of the dialogue in the later seasons. At least&nbsp;
it made me ponder the meaning of this line to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:06.600
some degree instead of the dialogue instantly&nbsp;
evaporating from my memory with zero impact.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:17.520
Number 19: “I know death. He’s got many&nbsp;
faces. I look forward to seeing this one.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.760
This is just a simple line that I don’t think&nbsp;
needs a lot of explanation. It thematically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.360
gels really well with Arya’s character given&nbsp;
her consistent relationship with the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:31.080
of death. And I like how she phrases wanting to&nbsp;
meet the Night King as him being another one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:37.280
death’s many faces. That and Maise’s delivery&nbsp;
of the line is so cool and badass. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.680
I don’t think I need to explain&nbsp;
this much further, so let’s move on.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:50.080
Number 18: “You want a whore, buy one. You want&nbsp;
a queen, earn her.” Ok this may seem like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:55.600
controversial pick by even including this line&nbsp;
due to how cringey the delivery of it may be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.760
but the line itself I think is fine to good.&nbsp;
On one hand, I like the symmetrical structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:05.960
of these two lines in the contrast it generates.&nbsp;
Along with it being an empowering line for Cersei&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:10.200
that Lena Heady essentially gives her all since&nbsp;
this is the only decent line of dialogue she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.480
gets all season. But on the other, it feels like&nbsp;
David and Dan are trying really hard to get this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.320
line onto a t-shirt. It seems as though they are&nbsp;
trying to get twitter girls to go crazy over this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:25.280
line and use it as a mantra or something. Which&nbsp;
I think happened briefly. So because of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.200
it kind of breaks my immersion whenever Cersei&nbsp;
says this line, but it made an impact for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.960
reason. It’s a solid line that’s unfortunately&nbsp;
weighed down by different aspects. It has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:40.120
the same energy as I drink and I know things&nbsp;
where it seems like David and Dan tried really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:45.800
hard to make an iconic line. However, I like&nbsp;
Cersei’s line more than Tyrion’s in season 6.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:50.720
Number 17: “You think our house words are stamped&nbsp;
on our bodies when we're born and that's who we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.040
are? Then I'd be fire and blood too.” This&nbsp;
line isn’t anything special by any means,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:00.400
but I think it offers a decent counterpoint&nbsp;
to Tyrion and everyone’s perspective of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.720
prejudiced towards Dany for being a Targaryen.&nbsp;
And the reason why I wouldn’t say this is a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:09.120
counterpoint is because it’s not necessarily the&nbsp;
house words but more because she has access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:13.800
nuclear weapons. And she’s starting to go off&nbsp;
the rails mentally. That and this line doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.160
really apply to most houses because they’re&nbsp;
normally positive sayings. But at the very least,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:23.720
David and Dan actually utilize a literary&nbsp;
device for this line in saying that house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.880
words are stamped onto someone’s body. Which&nbsp;
to me generally elevates lines because literary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:34.280
devices or figures of speech elevate the&nbsp;
creativity of writing. The first 4 seasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:43.280
are filled to the brim with literary devices&nbsp;
like an example being “Your mind needs books&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.400
like a sword needs a whetstone.” So in general&nbsp;
literary devices bring lines out of a mediocre&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.080
level and imbue them with wit. So that’s kind&nbsp;
of why I’m including this line since David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:58.400
and Dan’s literary device usage was almost&nbsp;
non-existent throughout the later seasons.

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.760
Number 16: “Tell them winter came for house Frey”&nbsp;
Similar to Arya’s last line, this is a simple,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:09.880
but effective line. The main reason why I’m&nbsp;
including it is because she uses the word&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:15.240
winter over other more generic words like death&nbsp;
or misery. Winter instead is a clever way of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.560
still saying that while also being a thematic word&nbsp;
relating to the Starks. So with this line she’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.000
essentially communicating in a smart way that the&nbsp;
Starks have taken revenge on the Freys. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.800
this is at 16 because of how simple it is&nbsp;
and I don’t think it can really be higher.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:36.280
Number 15: “I don’t plan on knitting by the fire&nbsp;
while men fight for me” I think this is a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:41.400
line for Lyanna Mormont. It’s pretty much the&nbsp;
embodiment of her badass persona and communicates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.720
a strong feminist message through this single&nbsp;
line. It’s not elevated by any good wordplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.040
but I really like the snappiness of this line&nbsp;
and how it immediately makes lord Glover shut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.680
up. To me, it feels like an empowering line&nbsp;
that instantly changes the opinions of every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:00.880
lord about whether or not women should fight in&nbsp;
the Long Night. Although, you could also probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.160
attribute that to lazy writing in that this point&nbsp;
of potential debate ends right away. Everyone just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.400
accepts women having to fight in the great war and&nbsp;
it seems like this could be a point of conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:16.680
But heyyy, that’s just overall writing and doesn’t&nbsp;
actually affect the quality of this single line.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:23.440
Number 14: “Sometimes duty is the death of love.”&nbsp;
I feel kind of dirty for even including this line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:27.320
because it’s essentially cheating on David&nbsp;
and Dan’s part. They literally reverse one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.840
of the best lines in the earlier seasons&nbsp;
and call it a day. But on a technicality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:37.120
it is a good line. I’m just not going to put this&nbsp;
line any higher because of how cheap it feels. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.120
the reason why I’m not putting it lower is because&nbsp;
narratively it actually fits well here. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:47.760
Jon’s duty of needing to kill the mad queen&nbsp;
is inherently the death of love. So in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:51.520
it’s kind of used literally. Tyrion is&nbsp;
communicating in a smart way what Jon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.080
needs to do to save the realm by reversing&nbsp;
his quote. It works well in this moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:00.640
but this line obviously has the baggage of just&nbsp;
slightly changing up an old hit and re-releasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:06.560
it. And even then, I’d still put it over most&nbsp;
other lines of dialogue in the later seasons.

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:12.440
Number 13: “She’s always been good at using the&nbsp;
truth to tell lies” Now this is a good line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.040
but I really can’t think of that many&nbsp;
examples of Cersei using the truth to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.040
tell lies. She mostly just flat out lies to&nbsp;
people. Or using the truth as a base point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:25.640
to fabricate lies like what most people do.&nbsp;
Although, they are referring to Cersei using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:29.920
the truth of her being pregnant and our&nbsp;
heroes expecting her to value her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.440
but she lies about helping against the Night King.&nbsp;
So it’s technically true and the reason why I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:40.920
putting this line higher than others is because of&nbsp;
the solid oxymoron. She uses truths to tell lies;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.200
which is an interesting way to phrase that&nbsp;
statement due to those words being opposites.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.320
David and Dan could have communicated this&nbsp;
line of dialogue in a much more generic way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.680
but I’ll give them credit here that it&nbsp;
works well. Still though, I don’t want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.640
to give them too much credit because it’s an&nbsp;
okay line amongst a bunch of terrible ones.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:06:59.460
Number 12: “Thoros isn't here anymore, so&nbsp;
I hope you're not about to give a sermon.&nbsp;

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:02.640
Because if you are, the Lord of Light's gonna&nbsp;
wonder why he brought you back 19 times just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:07.800
watch you die when I chuck you over this fucking&nbsp;
wall.” Between a lot of different characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.920
I think Sandor Clegane still has some really funny&nbsp;
lines in the later seasons. Now there’s kind of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:17.640
balance in that should I be prioritizing whittier&nbsp;
lines that are smarter, or I should I also include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:22.360
some clever usages of humor. And I think that&nbsp;
balance pretty much rests on the fact as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:28.000
how funny the line of dialogue is and how smart&nbsp;
of insult it is. Which to me, I think this line&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:33.200
is pretty much perfect for the hound. His pure&nbsp;
annoyance towards Beric is always fun to watch and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:37.440
him threatening to ruin his ultimate importance&nbsp;
in the long night by chucking him over the wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.600
is priceless. I just imagine the Lord of Light&nbsp;
malding in the sky. That and I didn’t even realize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:47.880
it, but the hound actually uses a hyperbole&nbsp;
by saying Beric was brought back 19 times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:53.640
when it was actually 6. So that aids the humor&nbsp;
even more. Overall, a classically funny hound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:58.840
line and I tried my best to pick his more clever&nbsp;
insults instead of him just cursing at people.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:04.560
Number 11: “Sometimes nothing is the hardest&nbsp;
thing to do” Honestly, I think this is a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.200
good piece of advice. However, I think Tyrion is&nbsp;
in the wrong for saying it at this point in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:14.600
story. Since he’s essentially advising Dany to&nbsp;
let Jon, Ser Jorah, and other important figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:19.200
to die. And the army of the dead up until this&nbsp;
point don’t have any countermeasures against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:24.480
a dragon. So theoretically, Dany should not be&nbsp;
in any danger and it’s really weird how Tyrion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.240
is giving her this terrible advice. But going&nbsp;
back to the actual line, this is something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:34.200
Tyrion says that has some form of intelligence&nbsp;
to it. People oftentimes get antsy and will make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:38.760
blunders if they don’t thoroughly think about&nbsp;
their actions. So doing nothing sometimes can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:44.840
be very important. Or the option to choose to do&nbsp;
nothing can also be vital in certain scenarios.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:50.120
It’s just kind of unfortunate that Tyrion is using&nbsp;
it at a completely bad time, although in hindsight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:56.200
he was technically right since the Night King got&nbsp;
a dragon. So sure, that was the correct decision.

00:08:56.200 --> 00:09:06.840
Number 10: “This one’s been killed 6 times and&nbsp;
you don’t hear him bitching about it” In terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:11.560
of sheer quality of dialogue, I’d say this is&nbsp;
partially inferior to Sandor’s last quote at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:17.160
number 12, however, I think this is a genius&nbsp;
insult towards Gendry whinging. It perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:21.560
highlights the contrast in temperament&nbsp;
of Gendry’s whiny childlike response to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.560 --> 00:09:26.760
the situation by comparing him to a grizzled&nbsp;
warrior. It stops Gendry dead in his tracks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:32.080
as he re-evaluates his entire attitude from&nbsp;
a single line delivered by The Hound. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:36.920
also funny how they are making fun of Gendry&nbsp;
for reasonably being upset for almost dying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:42.720
but these men are so badass that he comes&nbsp;
across as a child. Which this line perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:48.240
captures that dynamic. That and like usual&nbsp;
Rory McCann’s delivery is always immaculate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:53.800
and you simultaneously feel the savageness&nbsp;
and pure irritation exuding from his voice.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:02.040
Number 9: “The Lords of Westeros are&nbsp;
sheep. Are you a sheep? No. You’re a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:08.320
dragon. Be a dragon.” This is technically a&nbsp;
few sentences, but I’ll bundle them together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:12.920
into one overall line that communicates&nbsp;
a single point. In terms of wordplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.920 --> 00:10:16.880
this is a pretty basic line and comparing&nbsp;
people to animals has been done in the past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.880 --> 00:10:21.360
and obviously better. But at least David&nbsp;
and Dan use a small ounce of creativity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:26.640
calling commoners sheep and Dany a dragon. Which&nbsp;
honestly, isn’t impressive, but the line carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:31.040
a lot of weight because of the significance of&nbsp;
its implication. Olenna is trying to convince&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.720
Dany to ascend and become her true self and&nbsp;
I think this line is a great conduit for that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:40.920
point. Along with Dianna Rigg having perfect&nbsp;
line delivery in every scene, she’s in. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:47.960
also probably elevates this line for me. Just her&nbsp;
saying be a dragon alone is iconic and badass.

00:10:47.960 --> 00:10:52.720
Number 8: “Kill a few hundred people, they make&nbsp;
you a lord. Kill a few thousand, they make you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:59.960
king. And then all your cocksucking grandsons can&nbsp;
ruin the family with their cocksucking ways.” I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:04.640
honestly kind of torn on this line. Because the&nbsp;
first half I really enjoy, but the second half I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:09.640
think is inferior and is still communicating the&nbsp;
same point and I technically should include it. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:14.800
addressing the second half first, David and Dan&nbsp;
regularly devolve into the lazy insults of cock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:20.360
jokes, which utterly lack any creativity. This&nbsp;
is also kind of weird because David and Dan have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:24.960
written some good insults in the later seasons&nbsp;
and then we have Bronn who is constantly saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:33.040
cock every other line. Maybe there’s a weak link&nbsp;
between the two D’s. So Bronn saying cocksucking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:38.120
grandsons and cocksucking ways I think is a&nbsp;
lazy way to write the line. Besides that though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:42.280
I really like the statement that Bronn makes&nbsp;
here. Especially how he phrases his first sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:47.480
in that most of these rich families have been&nbsp;
established from mass carnage and sheer violence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:52.040
An example being the Targaryens essentially being&nbsp;
nobodies in Westeros and then they rode in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:56.520
their dragons and rocked most of the population.&nbsp;
Then they turned into the strongest house in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:02.160
Westeros. So I think Bronn raises an interesting&nbsp;
topic through this line and is a good counterpoint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:07.280
about how Jaime is gatekeeping Bronn from wanting&nbsp;
to become a powerful lord. Even though this moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:12.640
is still devoid of logic since the show acts like&nbsp;
there’s no other lords that exist in the reach.

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:17.560
Number 7: “It's my fucking luck I end&nbsp;
up with a band of fire worshippers.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.560 --> 00:12:22.000
Now this may not be a juicy insult, but&nbsp;
I absolutely love the sheer irony of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.120
statement. It’s just really funny to me how&nbsp;
Sandor, a man whose fear in life is fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.680
ends up with Lord of the Light fanatics. So this&nbsp;
line I think calls out a funny scenario that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.680
may not have really been thinking about. There’s&nbsp;
not much else about this line that’s special since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:41.080
it’s a quick and funny ironic statement, but&nbsp;
heyyy every line doesn’t need to be that long.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:51.320
Number 6: “It’ll take a while with a sparing&nbsp;
sword” Now I know that quite of few of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.320 --> 00:12:56.200
last lines have been leaning towards humor, but&nbsp;
I’d argue that it’s easier to do humor than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.200 --> 00:13:01.560
is making intelligent lines with creative usages&nbsp;
of literary devices. But I actually really like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:06.000
this line from Tyrion and is both a smart way&nbsp;
to ease the tension between him and his brother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:10.400
and is also pretty funny. For a moment this&nbsp;
single line from Tyrion actually made me think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:15.760
he was the Tyrion from the earlier seasons. A&nbsp;
character who is quick, sharp, and funny and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.760 --> 00:13:20.880
kind of line I think perfectly suits him. It’s&nbsp;
a great response that serves multiple purposes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.320
while also making it seem like Tyrion was actually&nbsp;
himself again, so that’s why I’d put it this high.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:33.360
Number 5: “The perils of self-betterment” Yeah&nbsp;
this is the most popular good line from season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.360 --> 00:13:38.920
8 that you were probably waiting for. Although,&nbsp;
honestly, it feels kind of overrated because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.920 --> 00:13:44.000
how simple it is and the fact that the statement&nbsp;
made by the line isn’t that deep. But there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.600
really not much else competition for good lines&nbsp;
after going through the entirety of seasons 7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:54.440
and 8 again, so I guess this is the start of&nbsp;
our top 5. But to give the line some credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.440 --> 00:14:00.160
self-betterment in general is an arduous task even&nbsp;
though it may not seem like that sometimes. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:04.840
because of this, I think the line is inherently&nbsp;
an oxymoron in that something that’s seen as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:08.960
positive can be a peril to go through. Then&nbsp;
from a character perspective, it acts as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.960 --> 00:14:14.200
good payoff for both of these characters who have&nbsp;
made massive transformations into better people.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:20.960
Number 4: “But then Mormont saw them and you saw&nbsp;
them. And I trust the eyes of an honest man more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:25.200
than I trust what everybody knows.” Initially,&nbsp;
I thought this was a line that Tyrion said in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.960
season 2 episode 2 where Goer Mormont sends&nbsp;
him the raven about needing more men. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:34.560
he doesn’t actually say this and it still feels&nbsp;
like I heard this line somewhere else. So since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:39.000
I’m losing my mind and can’t find a place of&nbsp;
origin then I guess David and Dan actually came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:43.960
up with it. And if I’m wrong then this line&nbsp;
gets sent down 10 spots. Similar to Tyrion’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:48.560
clever and funny remark towards Jaime, this is&nbsp;
a rare later season moment where Tyrion could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:53.360
be interchangeable with his earlier season self.&nbsp;
Putting your trust in honest people and ignoring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:58.280
the noise of public knowledge that is sometimes&nbsp;
twisted into being misinformation is actually a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:03.360
good nugget of wisdom. Honorable people can be&nbsp;
more reliable for information and overall this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:07.560
is a good and smart Tyrion line. I would&nbsp;
put it much higher if it actually utilized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:12.480
some kind of figure of speech and David and&nbsp;
Dan tried harder, but at least it was witty.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:19.160
Number 3: “Give us common folk one taste of&nbsp;
power, we’re like the lion who tasted man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:23.920
Nothing is ever so sweet again.” Honestly,&nbsp;
this line completely took me by surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:28.000
because I didn’t even remember that this was&nbsp;
said by Varys. Probably because this scene is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.440
overshadowed by Dany and Jon’s meeting. But&nbsp;
I was kind of dumbstruck that David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:37.120
actually used a simile for once. Although I&nbsp;
wouldn’t say it was particularly outstanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:41.960
and still wouldn’t crack the brilliance of the&nbsp;
earlier seasons, but heyyyy it’s still good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:46.120
The main reason why I wouldn’t put this line&nbsp;
higher is the meaning behind it. It’s a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:50.800
simple statement of if you give someone power&nbsp;
then they’ll want more of it. Like that’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.800 --> 00:15:55.480
obvious thing that people know and at the very&nbsp;
least the line itself was finally elevated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.480 --> 00:16:01.600
a literary device of calling the common folk&nbsp;
the lion the tasted man. So good job I guess?

00:16:01.600 --> 00:16:07.520
Number 2: “Incompetence should not be rewarded&nbsp;
with blind loyalty” Varys two for two baby;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.520 --> 00:16:12.720
back to back lines. Now this line may not&nbsp;
include a strong usage of a figure of speech,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:17.160
but I think this is a really good line that&nbsp;
is smart, is very in character for Varys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.160 --> 00:16:21.600
and exposes a great topic for the nature of&nbsp;
serving kings and queens. Because there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:25.760
so many characters that get stuck serving&nbsp;
incompetent kings. Like that’s kind of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.760 --> 00:16:30.320
tragedy of Varys in that he’s a very skillful&nbsp;
servant, but every king he’s served has either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:35.840
been insane or not caring about ruling. Varys&nbsp;
with this line basically strikes Daenerys with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:40.560
a blunt hammer of truth and makes her realize&nbsp;
that he in fact is even more loyal to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:45.480
because he genuinely believes in her. As long&nbsp;
as she’s competent and is serving the realm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:50.600
then that in turn breeds unbreakable loyalty.&nbsp;
So to me, this is Varys’ best line in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:55.560
later seasons and I actually think this is&nbsp;
Varys’ last good scene in the show as well.

00:16:55.560 --> 00:17:04.080
Number 1: “There’s always lessons in failures.&nbsp;
You must be very wise by now.” This may be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.080 --> 00:17:08.680
little underwhelming if you were expecting&nbsp;
some kind of intellectual big IQ line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:14.240
but those fundamentally don’t exist in the later&nbsp;
seasons so I have to make do. But this line from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:19.120
Olenna was such a genius and devastating insult&nbsp;
that it genuinely floored me. I think it’s by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.120 --> 00:17:24.520
far one of the most clever lines in seasons 7&nbsp;
and 8 and I think it easily cuts the deepest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.520 --> 00:17:29.080
Just with these few words, she states how much&nbsp;
of a failure Jaime is and it’s also done with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:33.840
a sarcastic twist of the line superficially&nbsp;
seeming like a compliment. A perfect line for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:37.760
Olenna as a character that is more subtle&nbsp;
and communicated with a delicious amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:42.560
of sass. Also, this kind of insult isn’t as&nbsp;
bombastically annoying as a lot of other more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:48.000
crude insults that David and Dan write. Like&nbsp;
I love The Hound’s spouting vitriolic insults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:53.200
but they normally lack creativity since David and&nbsp;
Dan just slap a couple curse words together. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:57.760
Tyrion or Bronn says cock and starts laughing&nbsp;
as if they came up with comedy gold. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.760 --> 00:18:02.280
this is my favorite line and you can probably&nbsp;
argue for the few lines before this being better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:07.360
but honestly, I don’t think really any of the&nbsp;
quote-on-quote intelligent lines are that good.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:12.000
After going through all of seasons 7 and 8 I&nbsp;
thought there would be some better hidden gems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:15.560
but this was really the best that there&nbsp;
was. It’s really sad that there’s this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:18.520
huge of a disparity in quality of&nbsp;
writing, probably because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:23.480
dealing with a world-class writer and then&nbsp;
two mediocre screenwriters. But even then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:28.600
David and Dan had years to write these scripts&nbsp;
and had brilliantly complex characters at their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:33.720
disposal. So I’m kind of surprised they couldn’t&nbsp;
write anything better for these characters. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:38.040
seems like a lot of dialogue is rushed into&nbsp;
just being used as a device to quickly move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:43.560
the plot forward. Instead of a wonderful tool to&nbsp;
investigate these characters in great depth. God&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:48.600
damn what a disservice to these characters and us.&nbsp;
Thank you for watching and my letterboxd, patreon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.600 --> 00:18:52.680
and discord link are in the description. And let&nbsp;
me know in the comments if I missed any lines from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:58.360
the later seasons that you liked or what your&nbsp;
favorite is. Also thank you so much to Logan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:05.160
Farmer for being a monumental patron and unlocking&nbsp;
tier of me shouting him out. Thank you so much.

